  "proof": {
    "notation": "n_p = clean phase size; k_p = WD-defined cells in that phase; B = total clean alarm budget.",
    "upper_bound": "For either WD polarity as a singleton OR clause, coverage_p <= min(n_p, k_p + B) / n_p. This generously gives each phase the entire shared budget.",
    "necessary_unknown_alarm_lower_bound": "sum_p max(0, ceil(0.8*n_p) - k_p)",
    "stronger_total_alarm_lower_bound": "necessary_unknown_alarm_lower_bound + number_of_known_WD_T_clean_rows_after_polarity",
    "reason": "At a row where the included WD clause is U, an OR can never be F. It is defined only if another clause is T; then the model raises a clean alarm. Every covered WD-unknown clean row therefore consumes one unit of the shared clean alarm budget.",
    "counterexample_boundaries": [
      "This claim applies to the frozen primary OR of singleton literals. An AND that masks U is a different grammar and is not evaluated or authorized here.",
      "Removing the new WD clause, changing U handling, changing the coverage definition, changing train population or relaxing clean/coverage budgets changes the problem. No such modification was performed.",
      "The proof does not say the entire combination group has no feasible model; it can select an OR without the new WD clause.",
      "The proof establishes a train-constraint obstruction, not detector performance or truth of attack labels."
    ]
  },
